      "content": "The higher concentration of minority populations in Zones B and C is likely to lead to instability and property value volatility due to a range of factors. For one, minority neighborhoods often have lower levels of economic investment and social capital, which can make them more vulnerable to external pressures from gentrification. Additionally, minority neighborhoods may have different cultural and social norms that can be at odds with the more affluent and homogeneous neighborhoods that developers often target.\n\nIn Zones B and C, the higher proportion of Black residents is particularly concerning, as historically, Black neighborhoods have been subject to disinvestment and neglect. This legacy of disinvestment can make it more difficult for these neighborhoods to recover from gentrification, as they may lack the economic and social resources to compete with more affluent areas.\n\nFurthermore, the lower median incomes in Zones B and C also suggest a lack of disposable income among residents, which can limit their ability to afford the increased costs associated with gentrification, such as higher housing costs and rents. This can lead to displacement and instability, as long-time residents are priced out of their own neighborhoods.\n\nIt's worth noting that these factors are not unique to minority neighborhoods, and that other demographic indicators, such as poverty rates and education levels, can also contribute to instability",
